      "era": "2024-2026",
      "title": "Thalapathy's Leap: From Silver Screen to Political Stage",
      "narrative": "The year 2024 marked a seismic shift in Tamil Nadu's political landscape as Joseph Vijay Chandrasekhar, affectionately known as 'Thalapathy' to millions, formally announced his entry into active politics. On February 2, 2024, Vijay launched the Tamilaga Vettri Kazhagam (TVK), declaring his intention to contest the 2026 Assembly elections and signaling a definitive departure from his illustrious film career. This move, long anticipated by his fervent fanbase, instantly injected a new dynamic into the state's traditionally bipolar political arena. The party wasted no time in establishing its foundations, applying for registration with the Election Commission of India (ECI) on the very day of its launch. By September 8, 2024, TVK secured its official registration, gaining the crucial legitimacy needed to participate in electoral politics.\n\nVijay articulated a clear ideological stance for TVK, positioning it as a centre-left party committed to egalitarianism, secularism, and social justice. He drew inspiration from towering figures like Periyar, Ambedkar, and Kamaraj, while advocating for a two-language policy (Tamil and English) and firmly rejecting right-wing communal politics. The party's slogan, 'Pirappokkum Ella Uyirkkum' (All are equal by birth), encapsulated its core philosophy. Though TVK opted out of the 2024 Lok Sabha elections, choosing not to contest or align with any bloc, its presence was felt through a massive inaugural political conference in Vikravandi on October 27, 2024, which reportedly drew over 800,000 attendees. Here, Vijay publicly challenged both the BJP as an 'ideological opponent' and the DMK as a 'political adversary' due to alleged corruption and dynastic politics.\n\nThe journey was not without its trials. In April 2025, a fatwa was reportedly issued against Vijay by the All India Muslim Jamaat, citing alleged anti-Islamic depictions in his films. A far more tragic event unfolded on September 27, 2025, when a crowd crush at a TVK rally in Karur claimed 41 lives and injured over 100, casting a somber shadow over the party's preparations. Despite these challenges, TVK pressed on, launching a massive enrollment drive in February 2025 to recruit 70,000 booth agents for the upcoming 2026 Assembly elections. As the 2026 elections approached, Vijay declared TVK would contest all 234 seats independently, releasing its manifesto and commencing his campaign from Perambur on March 30, 2026. The election results, unfolding in early May 2026, delivered a stunning debut: TVK emerged as the single largest party, securing 108 out of 234 seats, a remarkable feat that disrupted decades of Dravidian dominance, though it fell short of a clear majority. Vijay himself won from both Perambur and Trichy East, solidifying his position as a formidable new force in Tamil Nadu politics.",
